# Frontend Design and UX

## Design direction
- Build a coherent, modern interface appropriate for a TF2 jumping community resource hub.
- Support adaptive light and dark themes. Respect the user's saved preference and provide a sensible system-preference default where appropriate.
- Use a consistent design-token system for color, typography, spacing, radii, borders, elevation, focus, and motion.
- Prefer reusable shared components and patterns over duplicated page-specific implementations.
- Keep information density balanced: show important comparisons and status clearly, while using progressive disclosure for secondary or advanced details.
- Avoid decorative effects that reduce readability, performance, or accessibility.

## Responsive behavior
- Treat desktop and mobile as equally important product experiences.
- Adapt navigation to the available space and input method; do not simply shrink desktop navigation.
- Ensure core tasks remain discoverable and usable on touch devices.
- Support narrow viewports down to 320 CSS pixels without loss of essential content or horizontal page overflow.
- Rework wide tables into accessible compact layouts or controlled horizontal scrolling with clear affordances. Do not discard important columns or meaning on mobile.
- Respect safe-area insets where relevant.
- Test filters, menus, dialogs, cards, tables, and navigation at representative mobile, tablet, and desktop widths.

## Accessibility
- Target WCAG 2.2 AA.
- Use semantic HTML and a logical heading hierarchy, including one clear page-level heading.
- All interactive controls must be keyboard operable, have accessible names, visible focus indicators, and appropriate state semantics.
- Associate form labels and validation messages with their controls.
- Do not communicate status, tier, rating, or errors by color alone.
- Maintain sufficient text and UI contrast in both themes.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; avoid unnecessary animation and motion-triggered disorientation.
- Use appropriate ARIA only when native HTML semantics are insufficient.

## Interaction and presentation
- Provide clear loading, empty, error, stale-data, and success states.
- Keep filters understandable and usable with keyboard, touch, and assistive technology.
- Make active navigation and selected filters visually and programmatically identifiable.
- Keep typography legible and controls comfortably targetable; prefer approximately 44 by 44 CSS pixels for touch targets where practical.
- Avoid layout shifts when asynchronous content loads. Reserve space for data and imagery when dimensions are known.

## Implementation
- Inspect the existing CSS and markup before introducing a new design system.
- Consolidate repeated values into tokens and repeated UI into shared components.
- Avoid broad global selectors and fragile styling coupled to DOM position.
- Do not introduce a CSS framework or component library without evaluating its maintenance and bundle cost and obtaining approval for a major dependency.
- Keep styles organized by tokens, base, layout, components, and page/feature concerns where the project structure permits.
